// Test fixtures for the text-drawing layer: a one-sprite table for the
// sprite-font and tint paths (txt_spr_*, txt_tint_*), and two pixel readers
// so a case can witness a single drawn pixel without a `given` of its own --
// same reasoning as sf/boot/t.id's sf_t_gw/sf_t_gh.
//
// The sprite is 2x2, one frame, with only pixel 0 (row 0, col 0) opaque
// (colour 5); the other three stay (import d2_clear) from d2_spr_add's fill.
// That is enough to tell a blit's own colour apart from a tint's, and enough
// to prove a row or a frame boundary really stops the draw rather than
// happening to draw nothing.

txt_spr_t_setup() {
  d2_spr_init();
  int id = d2_spr_add("t", 2, 2, 1);
  d2_spr_put(id, 0, 5);
} return void;
():() then d2_spr_t_w_len:(1)
():() then d2_spr_t_f_len:(1)

// The surface pixel at (0, 0) is read back through d2_blit_t_px00
// (plane/spr/tab/init/fx.id) -- same body, so a second reader here would be
// a duplicate-logic error; every case in this layer that draws anything is
// arranged to land its one checked pixel there.

// The surface pixel at (1, 0), the immediate neighbour -- used to prove a
// draw touched exactly one pixel and not its row-mate.
txt_t_px10() {
  int c = sf_pget(1, 0);
} return int c;
given sf_t_setup ():(0)
given sf_t_setup ():(0) then sf_t_gh:(48)
